Lab 4: Managing Nova (Compute Service)
Objectives
Install and configure Nova services in OpenStack
Create and manage virtual machine instances
Monitor and troubleshoot Nova compute nodes and service status
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Keystone (Identity) service installed
Administrative access to the OpenStack controller node
Basic Linux command line proficiency
SSH access to compute nodes
Python 3.x installed
Lab Setup
Before beginning, ensure:

All OpenStack nodes have network connectivity
Database service (MariaDB/MySQL) is running
Message queue (RabbitMQ) is configured
Sufficient compute resources available
Task 1: Install and Configure Nova Services
Subtask 1.1: Install Nova Components
On the controller node:

# Install Nova API and conductor services
sudo apt install nova-api nova-conductor nova-novncproxy nova-scheduler -y

# Install Nova compute on compute nodes
sudo apt install nova-compute -y
Verification:

nova-status upgrade check
Expected Output: Should show no critical errors in the configuration

Subtask 1.2: Configure Nova
Edit the Nova configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/nova/nova.conf
Add/Modify these sections:

[DEFAULT]
transport_url = rabbit://openstack:RABBIT_PASS@controller
my_ip = MANAGEMENT_IP
use_neutron = true
firewall_driver = nova.virt.firewall.NoopFirewallDriver

[api]
auth_strategy = keystone

[keystone_authtoken]
www_authenticate_uri = http://controller:5000/
auth_url = http://controller:5000/
memcached_servers = controller:11211
auth_type = password
project_domain_name = Default
user_domain_name = Default
project_name = service
username = nova
password = NOVA_PASS

[vnc]
enabled = true
server_listen = $my_ip
server_proxyclient_address = $my_ip
Restart services:

sudo service nova-api restart
sudo service nova-scheduler restart
sudo service nova-conductor restart
sudo service nova-novncproxy restart
Task 2: Create and Manage Instances
Subtask 2.1: Verify Compute Service
openstack compute service list
Expected Output: Should show all nova services with "up" status

Subtask 2.2: Launch an Instance
First, source your OpenStack credentials:
source admin-openrc
Create a test instance:
openstack server create --image cirros --flavor m1.tiny --network demo-net --key-name mykey test-instance
Monitoring creation:

openstack server show test-instance
Subtask 2.3: Instance Management
Common operations:

# List instances
openstack server list

# Reboot instance
openstack server reboot test-instance

# Resize instance
openstack server resize --flavor m1.small test-instance

# Delete instance
openstack server delete test-instance
Task 3: Monitor and Troubleshoot
Subtask 3.1: Check Compute Service Status
nova service-list
Troubleshooting Tip: If any service shows as down, check logs:

sudo tail -f /var/log/nova/nova-compute.log
Subtask 3.2: Resource Utilization
openstack hypervisor stats show
openstack hypervisor list
Subtask 3.3: Common Issues
Instance not launching:

Check quota limits: openstack quota show
Verify network connectivity
Check compute node resources: free -h, df -h
Authentication errors:

Re-validate keystone configuration
Verify token expiration
Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Successfully installed and configured Nova compute services
Created and managed OpenStack instances
Monitored compute resources and troubleshooted common issues
Validation:

Verify all services are operational: openstack compute service list
Confirm at least one instance is running: openstack server list
Check hypervisor statistics: openstack hypervisor stats show
